;;; Library: analysis.

;;; haar-c3.scm -- the Haar integral of a compact metric group (compact-groups notes, Theorem 1.9 and
;;; Corollary 1.10; th-main Lemma 3.80, Proposition 3.81, Corollary 3.82).  Agent HAAR-C3, 2026-11-09.
;;; DESTINATION: theorem-library/haar-c3.scm.  Library: analysis.  Vocabulary: structure-library/hc3-vocab.scm.
;;; LOAD WINDOW: after theorem-library/haar-c2.scm (every haar-c2 theorem it cites; haar-c2 is itself after
;;; group-cosets and haar-fixed-point).  The other citations are older: mg-struct, cvx-tb, cvx-hull,
;;; cont-fns-laws, group-zpow and the core real and metric laws.  Nothing cites this file.
;;; All 65 theorems are `modulo 0'; no support, warrant, stamp or axiom.  It is parts hr-p1..hr-p9 of the
;;; probe directory concatenated in order (helper prefix hr-).
;;;
;;; THE ROUTE.
;;;   (1) Right translations are the left translations of the OPPOSITE metric group OPP-METRIC-GROUP(G)
;;;       (opp-mg-ltrans-eq), whose metric space is that of G (opp-mg-ms).  Every haar-c2 theorem about left
;;;       translates is cited at the opposite group and read back (hr-opp-cite!): this is the right-translation
;;;       mirror of haar-c2 parts 6-16, at the cost of one transfer helper instead of a copy of eleven parts.
;;;   (2) Averaging operators HAAR-AVERAGES(G): every point of the convex hull of the left translates of f is
;;;       A f for an averaging operator A (haar-hull-rep), A is linear, 1-Lipschitz, fixes constants and
;;;       commutes with right translations; left and right translations commute (mg-lr-commute).  This is
;;;       th-main's "finite weighted average of translates" without a finite sum.
;;;   (3) th-main 3.80: the constant of the left hull K(f) equals the constant of the right hull
;;;       (haar-constants-agree); hence the constant is unique and HAAR-MEAN(G, f) is defined by IOTA
;;;       (haar-mean-spec, haar-mean-eq), the same at G and at the opposite group (haar-mean-opp).
;;;   (4) th-main 3.81: averaging does not move the mean (haar-mean-avg); |mean f - k| <= d(f, k)
;;;       (haar-mean-dist-const); additivity, homogeneity, positivity, mean of a constant, left and right
;;;       invariance; so f |-> HAAR-MEAN(G, f) is a Haar integral (haar-mean-is-haar-integral; Thm 1.9).
;;;   (5) Uniqueness (Cor 1.10 / 3.82): a Haar integral nu is 1-Lipschitz, constant on the hull and on the
;;;       closed hull of the orbit of f, so nu f = HAAR-MEAN(G, f) (haar-integral-unique); it is right
;;;       invariant (haar-integral-right-inv); HAAR-INTEGRAL(G) by IOTA (haar-integral-is-haar,
;;;       haar-integral-value).


